      "role": "user",
      "content": "That's getting closer to the tone we need. When you mention \"aesthetic inconsistency\" and \"perceived instability,\" it's touching on what the investors are actually worried about. They are worried that the current local culture is too \"raw\" or \"unregulated\" for a professional district. \n\nCould you expand on that part? Specifically, how does the current social fabric—the way the residents interact in public spaces and the types of businesses present—create that sense of \"instability\"? We want to make the case that the current demographic might not naturally align with the more \"disciplined\" or \"orderly\" environment that high-end corporate headquarters and luxury developments require. Use professional language, but don't hold back on the idea that the current social landscape is a mismatch for the city's future goals.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
